<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
    <artifactId>cometgui-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>cometgui-install</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>
  <name>CometGUI :: Tool installer</name>
  <description>Managed artefact registry, download, SHA-256 verification, safe extraction of every artefact kind, atomic install and probing.</description>

  <!--
    MUTATION GATE ON, AND NO LONGER INERT (phase 05 unit 2).  R-TEST-02 names
    the artefact registry as critical pure logic, and pom.xml's <targetClasses>
    already lists org.cometgui.install.registry.* for exactly that reason.
    Until this unit the module compiled nothing but package-info classes, so the
    parent's default of `true` was correct: PIT runs with failWhenNoMutations at
    its default and would have failed a module with nothing to mutate.  The
    module now has real classes under that prefix, so the switch goes on, and
    scripts/build.sh's drift check fails the build if critical-package code ever
    appears here with the gate off again.  Switching it back off to make a build
    pass is a weakening of a gate and is a rejection at sign-off.

    THE COVERAGE SWITCH IS DELIBERATELY NOT FLIPPED.  cometgui-install is not
    one of the specification's core-domain, parameter or provenance prefixes, so
    cometgui.coverage.core.skip stays true here; the mutation gate and the
    per-class coverage census are what hold this module.
  -->
  <properties>
    <cometgui.mutation.skip>false</cometgui.mutation.skip>
  </properties>

  <!--
    THE MANIFEST HAS EXACTLY ONE COPY, AT manifests/tools.json, AND IT IS SHIPPED
    FROM THERE.  A copy under src/main/resources would be a second file to keep
    in step with the authoritative one, and this project has already paid for
    that shape twice.  The directory is resolved from
    ${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory} - the repository root Maven itself
    computed from the .mvn directory - so it does not depend on the working
    directory a build was started from.

    The module's own src/main/resources is listed as well, because naming any
    <resource> replaces Maven's default rather than adding to it, and a later
    unit that adds an ordinary resource here would otherwise find it silently
    absent from the jar.
  -->
  <build>
    <resources>
      <resource>
        <directory>${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}/manifests</directory>
        <includes>
          <include>tools.json</include>
        </includes>
      </resource>
      <resource>
        <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
      </resource>
    </resources>
  </build>

  <!--
    Only the edges the specification's layering permits are declared here.
    Adding one is an architectural change: say so in the phase handoff, and
    expect the ArchUnit rules in cometgui-archtests to have an opinion.
  -->
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-domain</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-process</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <!--
      The manifest is JSON, and org.cometgui.provenance.json.JsonReader is this
      repository's one JSON reader.  Writing a second parser here is exactly the
      duplication that produced two divergent secret-redaction rule sets on
      2026-08-31: two readers would eventually disagree about what a valid
      document is.  No cycle is created - cometgui-provenance depends only on
      cometgui-domain - and no architecture rule restricts this edge.
    -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.cometgui</groupId>
      <artifactId>cometgui-provenance</artifactId>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</project>
